2. Treat the reference prose and source below as baseline implementation and sample content. Instructions there such as exact, verbatim, preserve copy, original names or no new sections apply only to parts the user has not asked to change. Keep the visual composition and working motion unless a change requires adapting them.
3. Replace requested text wherever it appears: visible headings, navigation, buttons, repeated animated labels, accessible labels, title and description. Use the user's language and real information; never invent testimonials, qualifications, sales or results. Adjust wrapping and spacing for new copy, rather than clipping or forcing the old words. Keep selectors/IDs used by animation code; update their references consistently if necessary.
4. Use attached replacement media or supplied URLs in the requested slots. Update matching posters, responsive srcset entries, alt text, masks and data records where applicable. Keep the original asset for untouched slots. A public asset list is a list of defaults, not a requirement to reject user media. Text baked into an image/video/3D texture is not editable HTML: explain which asset needs replacing.
5. If assets.json or another download manifest manages a replaced file, update that entry and its downloader together so setup never restores the old asset. SHA-256 and byte sizes describe the original file: compute new values from actual downloaded bytes for a replacement; never invent hashes. For an attached local file, remove its old managed download entry and keep the new file in the project. Preserve valid checks for untouched assets. Do not change original hosted PrettySites files.
6. Recheck video duration, poster, crop, masks, camera bounds and animation timing after relevant media changes. Preserve playback controls, reduced motion and fallbacks. Media used by Canvas/WebGL must be local or CORS-enabled. Report a missing file or incompatible format clearly; do not silently replace requested media with stock images.
7. Run the project's existing build/checks where available and inspect desktop/mobile text, media loading, links, navigation and existing motion. Distinguish browser-local demo behavior from working server features. Report what changed and what you actually tested.

